<BaseLayout title="From the Volcano to the Beach — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Nine days in Hawaiʻi: Kīlauea erupting at night, Hilo and a Kona coffee farm on the Big Island, then a week of Oʻahu from Waikīkī to Pearl Harbor and the windward coast.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · September 2026</p>
  <h1>From the Volcano to the Beach</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Big Island lava glow, a Kona coffee farm, and a week on Oʻahu.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>9 days · September 7 – 15, 2026</strong><br />
    Kona → the Kaʻū coast → Kīlauea by night → Waikoloa → Hilo → Holualoa coffee country →
    Honolulu → Waikīkī and Ala Moana → the Capitol District → Pearl Harbor → East Oʻahu → Byodo-In → Kualoa →
    Haleʻiwa → home<br />
    Two islands · one inter-island hop · a rental car on each side ·
    47 photographs of the same red sky · one colony of parking-lot cats
  </div>

  <p>
    There was no occasion for this one. No anniversary, no conference, no long-laid plan: we decided to go, and the
    whole trip was planned the day before we left. Easy come, easy go. It turned out to be a good way to see Hawaiʻi,
    which rewards showing up more than it rewards preparation, and which on our very first night handed us something
    no itinerary could have promised.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trip has a simple shape. It starts with fire and ends with a city. The first half is the Island of Hawaiʻi,
    the Big Island, where the ground is young and black and in places still being made: we landed in Kona on Labor Day
    and that same night were standing on the rim of Kīlauea, watching the sky above the caldera glow like the inside
    of a furnace. The second half is Oʻahu, where nearly a million people live on an island you can drive around in a
    day, and where the view from Waikīkī is towers, surf and Diamond Head instead of lava fields.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the road-trip post, everything here is rebuilt from the camera roll: where and when each photo was taken,
    in order. Where the photos are silent, I've left the day quiet rather than invent something to fill it. Where a
    place has a story, I've added a little of it, because Hawaiʻi is a place where almost every name on the map means
    something.
  </p>

  <h2>Part I: The Big Island</h2>

  <h3>Kona, and the long way to the volcano</h3>
  <p>
    We landed at Kona a little after one in the afternoon. Kona's airport sits on a lava flow, and the first thing you
    notice stepping off the plane is that the landscape is not green but black: rough, crumbled ʻaʻā lava running down
    to the sea on both sides of the highway, with tufts of pale fountain grass growing out of the cracks. It looks less
    like a tropical island than like a building site that nobody has finished. Geologically, that is roughly what it is.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first meal of the trip was a late Chinese lunch in Kailua-Kona: a whole steamed fish under a nest of ginger and
    scallion, a sizzling plate of vegetables, and lettuce wraps. It was the right fuel for what came next, which was a
    long drive.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0907.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0907.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 1: from the Kona airport to lunch in Kailua-Kona, south around the island to a Kaʻū coast lookout, then Kīlauea, and back to Waikoloa after midnight." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: Kona, around the south end of the island, to Kīlauea, and a very late return to Waikoloa.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-lunch.jpg'} alt="A table with a whole steamed fish topped with ginger and scallion, a sizzling plate of vegetables and lettuce wraps." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>First lunch, Kailua-Kona.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From Kona to the volcano you can go north and around through Hilo, or south and around through Kaʻū. We went
    south. The Māmalahoa Highway winds down the Kona coast, past coffee country, through the long dry emptiness of the
    southwest rift, and then swings east across the bottom of the island, the southernmost stretch of road in the
    United States. Ka Lae, South Point, is just off to the side, and the little town of Nāʻālehu calls itself the
    southernmost town in the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just past Nāʻālehu, around half past five, the highway climbs to a lookout above the Kaʻū coast. The view is all
    green and blue: a grassy slope falling steeply to a ragged black shoreline, white surf working along it, and the
    coast running away to the east under a sky that was clearing after rain. Kaʻū is the least visited district of the
    island, and it shows. There is almost nothing built on that whole coastline.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kau-coast.jpg'} alt="Green grassy cliffs dropping to a dark lava shoreline and blue ocean under scattered clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kaʻū coast, near Nāʻālehu.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kau-coastline.jpg'} alt="A roadside guardrail above a long green coastline curving away with white surf on black rock." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same lookout, looking east along the coast.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Kīlauea at dusk</h3>
  <p>
    We reached Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park in the last of the daylight. At 6:26 we were at the Kīlauea Overlook,
    where the land just stops and the summit caldera opens below. In the blue dusk it was mostly mist. The one clear
    thing in the frame is a gnarled tree on the rim, its branches black against a pale blue sky, the kind of tree that
    looks like it was drawn by someone who had only heard trees described. It has the look of an ʻōhiʻa, the tough,
    twisted native tree that is among the first to grow on new lava, and whose red lehua blossoms in Hawaiian tradition
    are tied to Pele, the goddess of the volcano.
  </p>
  <p>
    The summit is about four thousand feet above sea level, a different climate from the warm coast we had left that
    afternoon. And as the blue drained out of the sky, the clouds over the caldera started to turn a color clouds are not supposed to be.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-ohia.jpg'} alt="A twisted tree silhouetted against a misty blue dusk sky on the rim of the Kīlauea caldera." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Kīlauea Overlook, 6:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The sky on fire</h3>
  <p>
    By 7:18 we had moved along Crater Rim Drive to another viewpoint on the rim, and the sky was red. Not sunset red:
    the sun was long gone. This was light coming up from below, from lava somewhere down in the crater, reflecting off
    the clouds and the volcanic haze until the whole sky over the caldera glowed orange-red, as if someone had left the
    door of a furnace open. The rim and the scrub along it were pure black silhouettes against it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-first-glow.jpg'} alt="A black silhouette of trees and crater rim under a sky glowing deep red-orange." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>7:18 pm, the first photo from the rim.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Kīlauea is one of the most active volcanoes on Earth, and its summit crater, Halemaʻumaʻu, is in Hawaiian tradition
    the home of Pele. In recent years it has been erupting on and off, sometimes for weeks at a time, sometimes in
    short, spectacular bursts. We had arrived on a night when it was very much on. Over the next hour the camera roll
    records forty-seven photographs of what is, technically, the same view. In my defense, the view kept changing.
  </p>
  <p>
    It changes because the glow breathes. The haze thickens and the sky goes a deep dull crimson; it thins and the
    color brightens to orange. And at the bottom of the frame, just above the black line of the rim, there are
    threads of real light: thin bright lines and spatters of incandescent lava, far down in the crater, so
    bright they burn out the camera's sensor into little white-yellow streaks.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-glow.jpg'} alt="The crater rim silhouetted under a glowing red sky, with a small bright patch of lava visible at the rim." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>7:30 pm: the glow, and the first visible lava.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-lava-lines.jpg'} alt="Thin glowing lines of lava visible below a red-orange sky full of illuminated haze." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>7:40 pm: lines of lava below the rim.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We were not alone. All along the rim, other people stood in the dark with their phones raised, small glowing
    rectangles held up against a glowing sky, all trying to catch the moment the color shifted again. There is
    something about standing on the edge of a crater full of molten rock, at night, that makes everyone take far too
    many pictures.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-rim.jpg'} alt="A tall frame of red sky above a black rim, with a faint line of lava light near the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>7:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-late.jpg'} alt="A red sky over silhouetted shrubs, with a bright streak of lava just above the rim." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>8:03 pm, still going.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo is from 8:15. Then came the long drive back across the island in the dark, to the Kohala coast. The next photo on the
    phone is a view over the resort lagoons at Waikoloa, lit up and silent, at sixteen minutes past midnight. It was a
    long first day. Everything after it was going to feel a bit tame.
  </p>

  <h3>Waikoloa</h3>
  <p>
    Our base for the Big Island was a resort at Waikoloa, on the dry Kohala coast north of Kona. Daylight showed what
    we had arrived at in the dark: a resort large enough to need its own transport system. A tram glides along a track
    between the buildings, and boats run along a canal, past red ti plants and lagoons. There was also a pool with an
    artificial waterfall under the palms, and for a moment it was very hard to remember we had been standing on a
    volcano twelve hours earlier.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-canal.jpg'} alt="A resort canal with a tram track alongside, red ti plants in the foreground and a curved hotel building behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikoloa: the canal and the tram.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-waterfall.jpg'} alt="Palm trees around a lagoon with a rock waterfall, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Palms and a waterfall, morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Across the island to Hilo</h3>
  <p>
    Just before eleven we set off up Waikoloa Road, bound for the other side of the island. The Big Island has two climates that barely seem to belong to the same place. The Kohala coast
    is a desert of lava and dry grass. The Hilo side, an hour and a half away, catches the trade winds and their rain,
    and it is lush, green and wet. Hilo is often called one of the rainiest cities in the United States.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0908.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0908.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 2: from Waikoloa across the island to the Hilo bayfront and downtown Hilo, then back to Waikoloa for shave ice." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: across the saddle to Hilo and back.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    By half past twelve we were on the Hilo bayfront, near the Moʻoheau Bandstand, with a tropical fruit haul lined up
    along a bench: dragon fruit, a pineapple, mangoes, papaya, passion fruit, and a large green pear-shaped fruit I
    still can't name with confidence. The bayfront itself has a sad history worth knowing. Hilo Bay funnels tsunamis,
    and the waves of 1946 and 1960 destroyed the old waterfront district. Afterwards the city chose not to rebuild on
    the lowest ground, and the shoreline became the long strip of park and open lawn that is there today.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-fruit.jpg'} alt="A row of tropical fruit on a bench: dragon fruit, mangoes, papaya, a pineapple and a green pear-shaped fruit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hilo bayfront fruit lineup.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next half hour is mostly a lot of photos of mango. A ripe Hawaiian mango eaten on a bench with the bay behind
    you is messy and perfect, and the pineapple came after it, cut into wedges. I will spare you the full sequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The early afternoon took us around Banyan Drive on the Waiākea peninsula, the road lined with enormous banyan
    trees, many of them planted from the 1930s onward by visiting celebrities: Babe Ruth and Amelia Earhart are among
    the names on the little plaques. Then came a late plate lunch in downtown Hilo, on Keawe Street. The plate lunch is
    Hawaiʻi's great working-class contribution to world cuisine: a scoop of rice, a salad, and whatever meat is
    on offer, all on one plate. Ours had a long glossy strip of short rib, chicken in red sauce, and salad.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-plate-lunch.jpg'} alt="A plate lunch of grilled short rib, rice, salad and chicken in red sauce, with a second plate of vegetables behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Plate lunch, downtown Hilo.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Back on the Kohala side by late afternoon, dessert was a cup of shave ice buried under a scoop of purple ube ice
    cream and a swirl of whipped cream, with a candy-striped straw. Hawaiian shave ice is not a snow cone. The ice is
    shaved so fine it is closer to fresh snow, and it soaks up the syrup instead of letting it pool at the bottom.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'ube-shave-ice.jpg'} alt="A clear cup of shave ice topped with purple ube ice cream and whipped cream, with a red-striped straw." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ube on shave ice, Waikoloa.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Kona coffee country</h3>
  <p>
    The third day began slowly, with a balcony view over green gardens and the ocean, a late morning on the resort
    grounds, and, at a quarter to one, iced coffee and an açaí bowl topped with fruit, toasted coconut and an
    orchid. Then we drove south, up into the hills above Kona, into coffee country.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0909.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0909.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 3: coffee in Waikoloa, a coffee farm in Holualoa, the drive back up Queen Kaʻahumanu Highway, sunset on the shore, a parking lot of cats, and dinner at ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: up to Holualoa, back for sunset, dinner by the bay.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Breakfast views and a coffee stop are about as much as a slow morning needs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-gardens.jpg'} alt="A view from a balcony over landscaped green gardens and lagoons toward the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view on day three.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'acai-bowl.jpg'} alt="An iced coffee and an açaí bowl topped with berries, coconut and a purple orchid." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coffee and açaí, with an orchid on top.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Kona coffee grows in a narrow belt on the slopes of Hualālai and Mauna Loa, a thousand or two feet above the sea,
    and it is one of very few coffees grown in the United States. The belt works because of a daily rhythm: sunny
    mornings, clouds rolling in over the slopes in the afternoon to shade the trees, and rich volcanic soil underneath.
    By the time we reached Holualoa, just before three, the afternoon clouds had arrived on schedule. The uplands were
    misty and gray-green, with a red-roofed farmhouse among the trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'holualoa-uplands.jpg'} alt="A red-roofed farmhouse among trees on a misty, overcast slope, with red earth in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Holualoa, in the afternoon clouds.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At <strong>Heavenly Hawaiian Kona Coffee Farms</strong> in Holualoa we saw how the beans are made, and it is far more involved than you would guess
    from a cup. Coffee starts as a fruit: a small red cherry. The cherries go into a pulper,
    which strips off the skin and fruit, and what is left are the beans in a papery husk called parchment. The farm's
    pulping deck was piled with burlap sacks, and a wooden box beside the machine was heaped with glossy red cherries
    waiting their turn.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-cherries.jpg'} alt="A wooden box full of red coffee cherries beside burlap sacks on a green-painted wooden deck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coffee cherries waiting for the pulper.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After pulping and washing, the parchment coffee is spread out to dry in the sun on a big wooden deck called a
    <em>hoshidana</em>, a Japanese word. Japanese immigrant families farmed much of Kona's coffee through the
    twentieth century, and the drying decks, with roofs on rails that can roll over them when it rains (which on
    a Kona afternoon is often), are their legacy. The farm's deck was covered in pale beans raked into long wave
    patterns, like a Zen garden made of coffee. Below it, the orchard rolled away down the hill.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-drying-deck.jpg'} alt="A covered drying deck spread with pale parchment coffee beans raked into wave patterns." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Parchment coffee drying on the deck.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coffee-orchard.jpg'} alt="Green rows of coffee trees on a hillside under gray clouds, with a small red-roofed building." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The orchard below.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Lava fields and a pebble heart</h3>
  <p>
    The drive back north on the Queen Kaʻahumanu Highway, named for the favorite wife of Kamehameha I, who went on to
    rule as regent and became one of the most powerful figures in Hawaiian history, runs for miles across old lava
    flows. At five in the afternoon the clouds were low and heavy over the mountains, and the road ran straight
    through a landscape of dark lava, pale grass and scrubby green trees, with rain hanging in gray curtains on the
    slopes ahead.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'queen-kaahumanu-hwy.jpg'} alt="A two-lane highway curving across dark lava fields with dry grass and green scrub under a low gray sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Queen Kaʻahumanu Highway, 5:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By a quarter to seven we were on the shore at Waikoloa. Someone before us had made a heart out of black lava
    pebbles on the wet sand at the water's edge, and the gentle wash was slowly rearranging it. On this coast the
    beaches are made of two kinds of stone, white coral rubble and black lava, and the lava pebbles show up on the light
    sand like pepper. We had been in the water with snorkel masks, and were still wearing them pushed up on our
    foreheads when the sun started to go.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'lava-pebble-heart.jpg'} alt="A heart shape made of small black lava pebbles on wet gray sand at the water's edge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Someone's lava-pebble heart.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then the sunset, which on the Kohala coast is something like a nightly event that people gather for. The clouds
    that had sat over the mountains all afternoon had broken up over the sea, and the sun went down behind them in a
    smear of orange and gray. Ten minutes later everything had gone pastel: a pink sky, lilac water, the surf
    breaking white over the black reef, and a beach of pale coral pebbles in the foreground.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-sunset.jpg'} alt="A dramatic sunset under heavy gray clouds over a rocky shore with palms and a twisted tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset, 6:50 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-sunset-surf.jpg'} alt="Pink and lilac sky over the ocean, with surf breaking over black lava rocks and a white coral-pebble beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A minute later, everything pink.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The cats of Waikoloa</h3>
  <p>
    The strangest scene of the trip came after dark. In a parking lot just off Waikoloa Beach Drive, around eight,
    the lights revealed cats. Not one or two. Dozens of them, spread across the asphalt between the painted lines:
    tabbies, black-and-whites, gingers, calicos, some eating, some washing, some just sitting in the middle of a
    parking space as if they had paid for it. Feral cats are a fact of life in Hawaiʻi, and this was clearly a well-
    established colony with a regular dinner hour. When we came back past the same spot nearly two hours later, they
    were still there, peering out from under the hedge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cats-parking-lot.jpg'} alt="Dozens of cats scattered across a dark parking lot at night, lit by headlights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cat parking lot, 8:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cats-colony.jpg'} alt="A wide view of many cats spread across an empty parking lot at night, with a hedge behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Every space taken.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Dinner in between was at Lava Lava Beach Club on ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay, a beach with old Hawaiian fishponds behind it.
    We ate by lamplight: ahi poke with purple taro chips and lime, served in a
    split-bamboo boat, a bowl of soup, and drinks with paper straws. Dessert was a tall coconut cake shaggy with
    shredded coconut, topped with an orchid. Hawaiʻi puts an orchid on everything, and it never gets old.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ahi-bamboo-boat.jpg'} alt="Ahi poke with purple taro chips in a split-bamboo boat on a wooden table at night, with drinks and a bowl of soup." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ahi in a bamboo boat, ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coconut-cake.jpg'} alt="A tall slice of coconut cake topped with an orchid, on a wooden table lit by a small lamp." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coconut cake, with orchid.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Part II: Oʻahu</h2>

  <h3>One short hop</h3>
  <p>
    On the morning of September 10th we left the Big Island. The view from the plane window at Kona, just before
    noon, sums up the island in one frame: a jet engine, black-patched tarmac, and behind the terminal the long, cloud-
    capped slope of Hualālai. The flight to Honolulu takes under an hour, barely long enough for a cup of juice.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0910.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0910.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 4: a flight from Kona airport on the Big Island to Honolulu airport on Oʻahu, then a drive to Waikīkī." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 4: the inter-island hop. Dashed blue is the flight.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-airport.jpg'} alt="A jet engine in the foreground on an airport tarmac, with the terminal and a cloud-covered mountain slope behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Leaving Kona, 11:41 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Honolulu's airport, officially Daniel K. Inouye International since 2017, has a secret that most travelers rush
    past: open-air gardens tucked between the terminals. Under huge spreading monkeypod trees there are paths, ponds,
    lava rock and tropical plants, and the old control tower with its "Honolulu International Airport" lettering rises
    over the trees. It is one of the few airports in the world where you can step out between flights into actual
    weather and actual birdsong.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hnl-garden.jpg'} alt="An airport garden courtyard with paths and tropical plants under a spreading monkeypod tree, with the control tower behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The gardens at Honolulu's airport.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hnl-monkeypod.jpg'} alt="A huge monkeypod tree with wide spreading branches over a garden, seen from a terminal walkway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A monkeypod over the garden.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Waikīkī from above</h3>
  <p>
    By early afternoon we were in Waikīkī, high up. From the top of a tower the whole famous view lays itself out:
    the crescent of the beach, the reef breaking white offshore with surfers waiting on it, the water in a dozen
    shades of turquoise and blue, the line of hotels along the sand, and at the far end, the long sloping profile of
    Diamond Head.
  </p>
  <p>
    Diamond Head's Hawaiian name is Lēʻahi. It is a volcanic tuff cone, and the English name comes from
    nineteenth-century British sailors who found glittering calcite crystals on its slopes and decided they were
    diamonds. They were not. Waikīkī itself is a remade landscape too: until the 1920s it was a wetland of taro
    patches, fishponds and duck ponds, and it was drained by digging the Ala Wai Canal, which turned the whole
    district into dry land you could build towers on.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-diamond-head.jpg'} alt="High view along Waikīkī's beach and hotels toward Diamond Head, with turquoise water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikīkī and Diamond Head from above.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-beach-above.jpg'} alt="Looking straight down on a sandy beach, a pier and turquoise reef water from a high tower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The beach, straight down.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That afternoon we had a visitor. A pigeon landed on the balcony ledge, looked around at the long drop below it with total indifference, and then hopped down to inspect the tiles for crumbs. It was the most
    photographed pigeon in Honolulu for about ten minutes.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'balcony-pigeon.jpg'} alt="A gray pigeon perched on a high balcony ledge behind dark railings, with towers in the background." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A visitor.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The lagoon at dusk</h3>
  <p>
    At seven we walked down to the Duke Kahanamoku Lagoon, a calm saltwater lagoon at the west end of Waikīkī, named
    for the Olympic swimmer who taught the world to surf. It was the blue hour. The water was perfectly flat and held
    the reflections of the palm trees and the lit windows of the towers, and along the horizon a thin band of pink
    sat over the line of palms. Nearby, on a lawn under the towers, a luau was getting ready to start, with long tables,
    torches and a stage. We also had a Hawaiian hot dog piled with jalapeños from a stand near the lagoon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lagoon-dusk.jpg'} alt="A flat lagoon at dusk reflecting a line of palm trees under a pink band of sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Duke Kahanamoku Lagoon, 7:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lagoon-dusk-tower.jpg'} alt="A lagoon at dusk with a lit tower and palm islet reflected in the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same lagoon, two minutes later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Dinner was Japanese, near the yacht harbor: a long piece of grilled unagi glazed with sauce on a black ridged
    plate, and a big platter of nigiri with tuna, salmon, scallop, shrimp, tamago and a hand roll. Japanese food is everywhere in
    Honolulu, a legacy of more than a century of Japanese immigration to the islands.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'unagi.jpg'} alt="Grilled unagi glazed with sauce on a long black ridged plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Unagi.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sushi.jpg'} alt="A platter of assorted nigiri sushi and a hand roll." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And the rest of the order.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Ala Moana</h3>
  <p>
    The next morning was clear and bright, with the reef glowing turquoise below and sailboats strung across the
    blue. We walked west along Ala Moana Boulevard toward Ala Moana Center, the enormous open-air shopping center
    that opened in 1959 and is still one of the largest of its kind in the world. Outside one of the shops a dark
    polka-dotted pumpkin sat in a shallow fountain, which is the kind of thing you do not expect to find next to a
    parking structure.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0911.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0911.jpg'} alt="Map of Days 4 and 5 on foot: Duke Kahanamoku Lagoon, Ala Moana Center, Ala Moana Beach Park, Magic Island and the yacht harbor view." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 4–5: the west end of Waikīkī and Ala Moana, on foot.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    From high up, the reef off Waikīkī is a map of itself: dark patches of coral, pale channels of sand, and a white
    seam of surf along the outer edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-reef.jpg'} alt="View from a high balcony over turquoise reef water, a beach and sailboats under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Morning over the reef.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ala-moana-pumpkin.jpg'} alt="A large dark pumpkin sculpture covered in polka dots, standing in a shallow pebbled fountain outside a shop." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A polka-dot pumpkin at Ala Moana Center.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Lunch was agedashi tofu under a heap of bonito flakes that waved in the heat like they were alive, which is what
    bonito flakes do, and which is either charming or alarming depending on who you are.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'agedashi-tofu.jpg'} alt="Fried tofu topped with bonito flakes and green onion on a black plate, on a rustic wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tofu and bonito flakes.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    After a mid-afternoon break, we crossed to <strong>Ala Moana Beach Park</strong>. This is where people who live in
    Honolulu go to the beach: a long, calm strip of sand protected by a reef, with big shade trees, picnic tables and a
    lagoon, and much less of Waikīkī's crush. At its east end is Magic Island, a peninsula that was built from
    landfill in the 1960s, intended for a resort that never happened. It became parkland instead, ringed by a calm
    swimming lagoon and a walk along the rocks.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ala-moana-park.jpg'} alt="A calm lagoon with grassy banks in Ala Moana Beach Park, with Waikīkī towers and a large tree behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ala Moana Beach Park.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kakaako-skyline.jpg'} alt="A row of glass towers along the shore across clear turquoise water, seen from Magic Island." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kakaʻako towers from Magic Island.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the tip of Magic Island the views go both ways. To the west is the new skyline of Kakaʻako, glass towers
    lined up along the beach like a city that grew overnight, which it nearly did. To the east, across the mouth of the
    Ala Wai Yacht Harbor and its forest of masts, is Waikīkī, and behind it Diamond Head again. We sat on the grass for
    a while with music playing on a phone. The water in the channel was so clear you could see the rocks on the
    bottom.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yacht-harbor-diamond-head.jpg'} alt="Waikīkī's hotels and the yacht harbor across blue water, with Diamond Head at the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikīkī and Diamond Head across the harbor mouth.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The sun went down that evening behind the towers at the west end of Waikīkī, a gold disc sinking into a sky full
    of small scattered clouds, with sailboats out on the water catching the last of the light.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-sunset.jpg'} alt="A golden sunset over the ocean with small clouds, beside the silhouettes of Waikīkī hotel towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the west end of Waikīkī.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Capitol District</h3>
  <p>
    Saturday began with another view, this one over the green lawns and trees of Fort DeRussy, a slice of Army land
    that has been kept as open space in the middle of Waikīkī, with the ridges of the Koʻolau range behind the towers.
    In the afternoon we drove into downtown Honolulu.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0912.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0912.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 6: from Waikīkī to the Hawaiʻi State Capitol, then to Pearl Harbor and food trucks in Waipahu, and back." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 6: the Capitol District, Pearl Harbor and Waipahu. The North Shore at dusk left no photos, so it isn't on the map.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fort-derussy.jpg'} alt="A high view over the green trees and lawns of Fort DeRussy toward Waikīkī's towers and the sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fort DeRussy and Waikīkī, morning.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The <strong>Hawaiʻi State Capitol</strong> is one of the most unusual capitols in the country, and it is full of
    symbolism. Completed in 1969, a decade after statehood, it has no dome. Instead, the building is open to the sky in
    the middle, like the caldera of a volcano. The two legislative chambers are cone-shaped, like volcanoes, the forty
    tall columns around it are meant to suggest royal palms, and the whole building stands in a reflecting pool that
    represents the ocean surrounding the islands. A giant bronze state seal hangs over each entrance, with the motto
    <em>Ua Mau ke Ea o ka ʻĀina i ka Pono</em>, "the life of the land is perpetuated in righteousness."
  </p>
  <p>
    In front of the Beretania Street entrance stands Father Damien, in a blocky, almost cubist bronze by the artist
    Marisol. Damien was the Belgian priest who went to live and work among the people exiled with leprosy to the
    Kalaupapa peninsula on Molokaʻi, caught the disease himself, and died there in 1889. He is now a saint, and one of
    Hawaiʻi's two representatives in the National Statuary Hall in Washington.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hawaii-capitol-damien.jpg'} alt="The open entrance of the Hawaiʻi State Capitol with tall columns, a large bronze state seal, and a statue of Father Damien in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Father Damien and the state seal, Hawaiʻi State Capitol.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Pearl Harbor</h3>
  <p>
    Half an hour later we were at Pearl Harbor. We kept to the shore this time, walking the grounds around the visitor
    center rather than taking the boats out, and that turned out to be plenty. The visitor center sits on the shore of the harbor, looking across
    the water at Ford Island, where the battleships of the Pacific Fleet were moored on the morning of December 7,
    1941. The attack that morning killed more than 2,400 Americans and brought the United States into the Second World
    War. It is a quiet place now, and the water is calm and gray-blue.
  </p>
  <p>
    Moored on the shore by the visitor center is the submarine <strong>USS <em>Bowfin</em></strong>. She was launched on
    December 7, 1942, exactly a year after the attack, and earned the nickname "the Pearl Harbor Avenger" for her
    war patrols in the Pacific. Her long gray hull lies low in the water beside the walkway.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uss-bowfin.jpg'} alt="The submarine USS Bowfin moored beside a walkway on Pearl Harbor under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>USS <em>Bowfin</em> (SS-287).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Across the harbor, on Ford Island, are the two ships that stand for the beginning and the end of America's war,
    and from the shore you see them the way the harbor itself does, at a distance across the water. The battleship <strong>USS <em>Missouri</em></strong>, gray and enormous, her hull number 63 visible even from the
    far shore, is where Japan signed the formal surrender in Tokyo Bay in September 1945. She was moored here as a
    museum ship so that she would look over the <strong>USS <em>Arizona</em> Memorial</strong>, out in the harbor
    a short boat ride from the visitor center: the white structure that spans the sunken hull of the battleship <em>Arizona</em>, where more than 1,100 of her crew died on the
    morning of the attack and most of them still lie. Between them stands the red-and-white control tower of Ford
    Island's old airfield.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'uss-missouri.jpg'} alt="The battleship USS Missouri moored at Ford Island across Pearl Harbor, with a red-and-white control tower and a Pearl Harbor Historic Sites sign in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The USS <em>Missouri</em> across the harbor, from the visitor center shore.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the lawn by the shore, a low eight-sided marker honors the Marines who served aboard the <em>Arizona</em>. One
    of its bronze panels lists the Marines of the ship's detachment who survived the attack; another reads "Semper
    Fidelis and Mahalo." It is easy to walk past, and worth stopping for.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'arizona-marines-memorial.jpg'} alt="A low octagonal stone memorial with bronze plaques honoring the USS Arizona's Marines, on a lawn by the harbor." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A memorial to the <em>Arizona</em>'s Marines.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From Pearl Harbor we drove on to <strong>Waipahu</strong>, an old sugar-plantation town where the Oahu Sugar
    Company's mill ran for almost a century. In a lot under spreading monkeypod trees, a row of food trucks was
    serving the early-evening crowd, among them the red-and-white trailer of Leonard's, the Honolulu malasada bakery.
    From there we kept going up to the North Shore, and at dusk we were at Ted's Bakery, which went unphotographed.
    That night, back in Waikīkī, the towers were lit up window by window, a vertical
    city of tiny yellow rooms, with the streets glowing far below.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waipahu-food-trucks.jpg'} alt="A row of colorful food trucks under monkeypod trees at the edge of a parking lot in Waipahu." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Food trucks, Waipahu.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-night.jpg'} alt="Waikīkī apartment towers lit up at night, with a street and a glowing pool far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikīkī, 9:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Around the east end</h3>
  <p>
    Sunday we left the driving to someone else. We had signed up for a day-long guided tour around the island, the
    kind where a bus collects you at dawn, a driver talks the whole way, and every famous view gets about ten
    minutes: out, look, photograph, back on the bus. It is not a way to know a place, but it is a very efficient way
    to see a lot of one. At 6:49 we were out in Waikīkī waiting for pickup, and by 7:23 the bus was on Diamond Head
    Road, which hugs the cliffs on the seaward side of the crater. The morning sun was low over the ocean, turning a
    long path of water to silver, and below the road the surf was rolling in along a narrow beach under the cliffs.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0913.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0913.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 7: from Waikīkī along Diamond Head Road to Koko Crater and the Hālona coast, over to Byodo-In Temple and Kualoa, then to Haleʻiwa on the North Shore and back." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 7: around the east end, up the windward side, and over to the North Shore.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'diamond-head-road.jpg'} alt="Morning sun glinting on the ocean below green cliffs along Diamond Head Road." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Diamond Head Road, 7:23 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Past Hawaiʻi Kai the highway climbs around the eastern tip of the island, through a landscape that suddenly looks
    volcanic again. Here the volcanoes are young by Oʻahu standards. <strong>Koko Crater</strong> rises beside the
    road, a steep tuff cone whose sides are scored by deep parallel gullies, so that in the low morning light it
    looks ribbed, like a pleated green skirt.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few minutes on, near the Hālona Blowhole lookout, the coast turns to raw lava shelf: dark, layered rock with the
    surf crashing over it, a long sandy beach curving away beyond, and the cliffs of Makapuʻu in the distance. It is
    the windiest, wildest bit of the loop, and it comes just half an hour from Waikīkī. We had a few minutes at the
    lookout, long enough for the wind to rearrange everyone's hair, and then it was back on the bus.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'koko-crater.jpg'} alt="A steep green volcanic cone with deeply ribbed slopes under a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Koko Crater, 7:44 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'halona-coast.jpg'} alt="A rugged dark lava coastline with surf, a long beach and green hills curving away under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The coast near Hālona.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Byodo-In Temple</h3>
  <p>
    From there the bus went over to the windward side and up the coast to the <strong>Byodo-In Temple</strong>, in the
    Valley of the Temples at the foot of the Koʻolau cliffs. We got there at nine, and a line was already forming. This
    was a photo stop, not a visit, so what we came away with is the view from the edge of the pond. The temple was built in 1968
    to mark the hundredth anniversary of the first Japanese immigrants to Hawaiʻi, and it is a replica, at smaller
    scale, of the Byōdō-in in Uji, near Kyoto, a Buddhist temple built in the eleventh century, whose Phoenix Hall
    appears on the Japanese ten-yen coin.
  </p>
  <p>
    The setting is what makes it. The red-and-white hall with its upswept roofs sits on the edge of a large pond, with
    a fountain and koi, and behind it, close enough to fill the whole sky, rise the Koʻolau cliffs: sheer, green,
    fluted walls of rock, deeply grooved by rain, with clouds snagged along the top. It looks like a Japanese
    painting that someone set down in front of the wrong mountains, and somehow the mountains suit it better.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'byodo-in.jpg'} alt="The red Byodo-In Temple beside a pond with a fountain, beneath tall pines and steep green fluted cliffs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Byodo-In Temple under the Koʻolau cliffs.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Kualoa, and the North Shore</h3>
  <p>
    Further up the coast, at <strong>Kualoa</strong>, the Koʻolau range ends in a spectacular row of jagged peaks
    above the flat green land by the sea. Kualoa is one of the most sacred places on Oʻahu, and these ridges and the
    valleys behind them, now a working ranch, will look familiar to anyone who has seen <em>Jurassic Park</em> or
    any of the many films shot here since. We didn't go into the ranch; the tour pulled over for the view, and the
    white shuttle in the photo below is a reminder of how most people see Kualoa. In the beach park across the road,
    the lawn runs down to the water under wind-bent trees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just offshore sits <strong>Mokoliʻi</strong>, a small, steep, cone-shaped island. Its name means "little lizard":
    in Hawaiian legend it is the tail of a giant moʻo, a lizard spirit, slain by the goddess Hiʻiaka, sister of Pele.
    It felt like a fitting end to the Pele thread that began on the rim of Kīlauea.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kualoa-ridges.jpg'} alt="Jagged green mountain ridges at Kualoa above palm trees, with a white tour shuttle bus parked on the grass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kualoa ridges.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kualoa-mokolii.jpg'} alt="A grassy park with wind-bent trees by the sea, with the small cone-shaped island of Mokoliʻi offshore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Kualoa Regional Park and Mokoliʻi.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By one o'clock the bus had come around to <strong>Haleʻiwa</strong>, the old plantation-era town that is now the
    capital of the North Shore. In winter this coast gets some of the biggest surf in the world, and Haleʻiwa fills
    with surfers and spectators. In September the sea is mostly calm, and the town is about shave ice, food trucks and
    surf shops. It was another short stop, and the camera roll has just one photo from there, of a parking lot, so
    I'll leave Haleʻiwa at that. The tour also made a brief stop at the Dole Plantation, in the pineapple country in
    the middle of the island, and then the bus took us back down to Waikīkī.
  </p>

  <h2>Leaving</h2>
  <p>
    Monday was quiet, at least in the camera roll: two photos from late morning on Saratoga Road in Waikīkī,
    banyan-shaded and bright, with the towers rising behind. Then, early on Tuesday, the sky put on a farewell. At
    6:36 the clouds over the Koʻolau mountains caught the sunrise and turned gold, while the city below was still in
    shadow, and over the rooftops we could see Diamond Head, with a pink building tucked among the towers below it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'saratoga-road.jpg'} alt="A sunny Waikīkī street with a spreading tree, shops and a tall glass tower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Saratoga Road, Waikīkī.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikiki-sunrise.jpg'} alt="Golden sunrise clouds over Waikīkī rooftops, with Diamond Head and a pink hotel in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunrise, the last morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two hours later we were in the air, heading east. Out of the window, past the wing, a long island lay in a
    deep blue sea, wrapped in its own little clouds, the islands sliding away behind us one by one.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wing-islands.jpg'} alt="A plane wing over a deep blue ocean with an island coastline and small clouds below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The islands from the air, September 15.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Two islands</h2>
  <p>
    Nine days is not much for two islands, and the two halves of this trip could hardly be more different. The Big
    Island was raw and enormous and nearly empty: fresh lava, long dark roads, coffee farms in the clouds, and a sky
    lit red by a volcano. Oʻahu was a city with beaches, a deep and sometimes painful history, and very good sushi.
  </p>
  <p>
    But they are the same story, told at different ages. Oʻahu is what the Big Island will become: its volcanoes
    went quiet long ago, the rain has carved its mountains into the fluted cliffs behind Byodo-In, and people have
    filled its lowlands with towers. The Big Island is what Oʻahu once was, still being built while we were there. Standing on the rim of Kīlauea, you watch land being made. In Waikīkī, you see what happens a few million
    years later. I would happily go back to either. Mostly, though, I want to see that red sky again.
  </p>
